tel 1.0

base ypsiloid
import soundness

info   YAML parsed into typed values and rendered back: documents, paths, codecs and `y"…"` literals
detail
    _Ypsiloid_ handles YAML the way Soundness handles JSON: text parses into a `Yaml` value,
    a `Yaml` decodes into a case class (or any type with a `Decodable in Yaml`) and encodes
    back from one, and the `y"…"` interpolator writes YAML which is checked as the code
    compiles. The parser covers the language as it is really used — block and flow styles,
    the three scalar quotings, block scalars with their chomping indicators, tags, anchors
    and aliases, directives and multi-document streams — and a `Yaml` renders back to clean,
    re-parseable block-style text with `show`.

    The central type is `Yaml`, a document: an immutable tree (`Yaml.Ast`) plus, when the
    document was parsed with position tracking on, an index from which any node's line and
    column can be recovered. A node within a document is addressed by a `YamlPath` (the same
    RFC 6901 escaping as a JSON Pointer, written `#/a/b`), which is how a decoding error says
    _where_ it happened, and how `locate` finds a node's source position.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import formatting.blockYamlFormatting

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    val alice: Person = t"{name: Alice, age: 30}".read[Yaml].as[Person]
    val same: Person = t"name: Alice\nage: 30".read[Person in Yaml]
    val text: Text = Person(t"Alice", 30).in[Yaml].show   // name: Alice\nage: 30\n
    ```

    Decoding is the derivation every other format uses (wisteria's, over `Decodable in Yaml`
    and `Encodable in Yaml`), so a record needs no annotation: `@name` renames a field or a
    variant on the wire, an enumeration encodes with a discriminator field named by the
    `discriminables` given in scope, and an `Optional` or `Option` field reads an absent key
    or a `null` as `Unset`/`None` under policies the `optionalityOptions` package varies.
    Under an accruing strategy (`validate`), a decode collects every fault in the document,
    each tagged with a `Yaml.Focus` — its path and, for a tracked document, its position —
    rather than stopping at the first.

    A record can also carry a _direct_ parser and printer (`Yaml.Parsable` and
    `Yaml.Printable`), which read fields straight off the parsed tree and write text with no
    tree built: `derives Yaml.Direct` derives both into the companion, and `import
    codecs.direct` selects the direct path for every record in scope (`codecs.derived` and
    `codecs.strict` are the other modes).

    The library has two source sets:

    - `ypsiloid.core` — `Yaml` and everything inside it, `YamlPath`, the `y"…"` and `yp"…"`
      interpolators, and the choice packages `formatting`, `optionalityOptions`,
      `discriminables`, `dynamicAccess` and `conversions`. It compiles for the JVM, Scala.js
      and Scala Native.
    - `ypsiloid.http` — the `postables`, `servables` and `construables` givens which let a
      `Yaml` travel as an `application/yaml` request or response body.

    Several givens are choices and must be imported by name, since `import soundness.*` does
    not import givens: `formatting.blockYamlFormatting` enables `show` and HTTP encoding,
    `dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml` enables `yaml.field` access and `yaml.field = value`
    updates, `discriminables.yamlByTypeDiscriminable` (or `…ByKind…`) is needed to encode or
    decode a sum type, and `parsing.trackPositions` (from zephyrine) turns position tracking
    on. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where the choice packages merge
    with those of the other formats.

refer ypsiloid.Yaml
refer ypsiloid.YamlPath
refer ypsiloid.y
refer ypsiloid.formatting
refer ypsiloid.optionalityOptions

entry .Yaml   a YAML document: an immutable tree, decoded with `as`, updated through lenses, rendered with `show`
  detail
      A `Yaml` wraps the parsed tree (`#root`, a `Yaml.Ast`) and, when it was parsed with
      `parsing.trackPositions` in scope, a `#positionIndex` from which `locate(path)` finds
      any node's line and column. It is produced by `text.read[Yaml]`, `data.read[Yaml]`, a
      `y"…"` literal, `value.in[Yaml]`, or `Yaml.make(field = …)`, and consumed by `#as`,
      which decodes it as any type with a `Decodable in Yaml`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.blockYamlFormatting
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val doc: Yaml = t"name: Alice\nage: 30".read[Yaml]
      doc.as[Person]                 // Person(t"Alice", 30)
      doc(t"age").as[Int]            // 30
      doc.age.as[Int]                // 30, with `dynamicYaml` in scope
      (doc.age = 31).as[Person]      // a new document; `doc` is unchanged
      doc.show                       // name: Alice\nage: 30\n
      ```

      A document is a value: every update (`#update`, `#updateDynamic`, a `lens`) builds a
      new `Yaml` and leaves the old one as it was. Two documents are equal when their trees
      are structurally equal (`Yaml.Ast.deepEquals`), whatever their positions.

      Reading: `.decodable` and `.aggregable` parse text, `.aggregableData` parses bytes
      without transcoding, and `.aggregableAll` reads a `---`-separated stream as a
      `List[Yaml]`. A stream source is parsed as it arrives, never concatenated first, so a
      large file parses without being held whole. Parsing raises `Parse.Error`, whose issue
      is a `Yaml.Ast.Issue` naming what was wrong and where.

      Decoding: `#as` and the primitive, collection, `Option`/`Optional` and derived codecs
      raise `Yaml.Error` — `Reason.NotType(found, expected)` for a value of the wrong shape,
      `Reason.Absent` for a missing field — and track a `Yaml.Focus`, so an accruing strategy
      reports each fault at its path. Shorthand: `text.read[Person in Yaml]` reads and decodes
      in one step, and `person.in[Text over Yaml]` encodes and renders in one.

      Rendering: `show` needs a `Yaml.Formatting` in scope (`formatting.blockYamlFormatting`,
      the only one) and emits block style — two-space indentation, `{}` and `[]` for empty
      collections, a string plain only when it cannot be mistaken for a number, boolean or
      null, double-quoted otherwise — chosen so that the text re-parses to the same tree.
      `.emit` renders a block at a time, and `.streamable` makes a `Yaml` a `Stream[Text]`.

      Dynamic access (`#selectDynamic`, `#applyDynamic`, `#updateDynamic`, `#update` and the
      `.lens` given) is gated on a `Yaml is Dynamical`, which `dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml`
      grants to a scope and `dynamically[Yaml]:` to a block, so that `doc.name` is never
      mistaken for a member of `Yaml` by accident.

      The fallback codecs every type reaches — `decodable`, `encodable`, `optional`,
      `optionalEncodable`, `encodableOver` and `aggregableIn` — are inherited from the
      priority layer `Yaml2`, and the direct-codec entry points (`aggregableParsed`,
      `encodableOverPrinted` and the two `codecs.strict` failures) from `Yaml1`; they are
      documented on those traits.
  refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Ast
  refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Error
  refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Focus
  refer ypsiloid.YamlPath
  refer ypsiloid.formatting
  entry #root   the document's tree, a `Yaml.Ast`
      The parsed tree, for the accessors in `Yaml.Ast`'s companion (`isObject`, `objectKey`,
      `arrayElement`, `string`, …) and for bridges to other tree formats. `Yaml.unseal` is
      the same node, as a named act of breaking through the abstraction.
  entry #positionIndex   the position index built by a tracked parse, or `Unset`
      Populated only when the document was parsed with `parsing.trackPositions` in scope;
      `Unset` for an untracked parse and for every `Yaml` built from a value. It is what
      `locate(path)` and `locateKey(path)` (zephyrine's `Positionable` extensions, through
      `.positionable`) walk to find a node's `Yaml.Ast.Position`.
  entry #as   decodes the document as any type with a `Decodable in Yaml`
      `doc.as[Person]` decodes through the `Person is Decodable in Yaml` in scope — derived
      for a case class or enumeration, or one of the companion's givens for primitives,
      `Text`, `List`, `Set`, `Map[Text, _]`, `Option` and `Optional`. It raises `Yaml.Error`
      and tracks `Yaml.Focus`: under `strategies.throwUnsafely` the first fault throws; under
      a `validate[Yaml.Focus]` block every fault is accrued, and each focus carries the
      path of the field (`#/address/city`) and, for a tracked document, its source position,
      which `#as` fills in for every registered focus before returning.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int, email: Text)

      t"name: Alice\nage: 30\nemail: a@b.c".read[Yaml].as[Person]
      capture[Yaml.Error](t"name: Alice\nage: thirty".read[Yaml].as[Person]).reason
      // Yaml.Error.Reason.NotType(Yaml.Primitive.Str, Yaml.Primitive.Integer)
      ```
  entry #apply   `doc(index)` is a sequence item; `doc(field)` is a mapping value, or absent
      `doc(1)` is the second item of a sequence, raising `Yaml.Error` with
      `Reason.NotType(found, Sequence)` when the root is not one. `doc(t"name")` is the value
      under a string key, or an absent `Yaml` (one whose root `isAbsent`) when the key is
      missing or the root is not a mapping; decoding an absent document as an `Option` or
      `Optional` gives `None`/`Unset`, and as anything else raises `Reason.Absent`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"[10, 20, 30]".read[Yaml](1).as[Int]            // 20
      t"{name: Alice}".read[Yaml](t"age").as[Option[Int]]   // None
      ```
  entry #selectDynamic   `doc.field`: a mapping value by name, with dynamic access enabled
      `doc.name` desugars to `selectDynamic("name")` and is `#apply` with a text key, so a
      missing key gives an absent document rather than an error. It needs a `Yaml is
      Dynamical` in scope: `import dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml`, or a `dynamically[Yaml]:`
      block.
  entry #applyDynamic   `doc.field(index)`: an item of a sequence-valued field
      `doc.items(1)` is `doc(t"items")(1)`: the field by name, then the item by index,
      raising `Yaml.Error` when the field is not a sequence. Needs a `Yaml is Dynamical`.
  entry #update   `doc(index) = value`: a new document with one sequence item replaced
      Encodes `value` through its `Encodable in Yaml` and returns a copy of the document with
      the item at `index` replaced; an index past the end leaves the sequence as it was. A
      root which is not a sequence raises `Yaml.Error`. Needs a `Yaml is Dynamical`.
  entry #updateDynamic   `doc.field = value` adds or replaces a mapping entry; `= Unset` removes it
      `doc.age = 31` desugars to `updateDynamic("age")(31)`: the value is encoded through its
      `Encodable in Yaml`, and the result is a new document in which `age` is replaced if
      present and appended otherwise. `doc.age = Unset` deletes the entry, and is a no-op for
      a key not present. A root which is not a mapping raises `Yaml.Error`. Needs a `Yaml is
      Dynamical`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml

      val doc = t"{name: Alice, age: 30}".read[Yaml]
      (doc.age = 31)(t"age").as[Int]           // 31
      (doc.age = Unset)(t"age").as[Option[Int]]   // None
      ```
  entry .apply   wraps a tree as a document: `Yaml(ast)` or `Yaml(ast, positions)`
      Builds a `Yaml` over a node, optionally with the `Yaml.PositionIndex` of a tracked
      parse. The parameter is typed `Any` because `object Yaml` extends `Dynamic` (for
      `.make`), which suppresses the constructor proxy; the value must really be a
      `Yaml.Ast`. `.ast` is the typed form for a node already in hand.
  entry .ast   wraps a `Yaml.Ast` node as a document
      `Yaml.ast(node)` is `Yaml(node)` with the node's type checked: the way the optics and
      codecs rebuild a document after replacing part of its tree.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Yaml.ast(Yaml.Ast.Integer(42))
      ```
  entry .unseal   the tree inside a document, as a named act of breaking the abstraction
      `Yaml.unseal(doc)` is `doc.root`: the `Yaml.Ast`, for pattern matching with the
      `Yaml.Ast.Sequence`, `Mapping`, `Str`, … extractors, or for comparing two parses with
      `Yaml.Ast.deepEquals`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Yaml.unseal(t"[1, 2, 3]".read[Yaml]) match
        case Yaml.Ast.Sequence(items) => items.length
        case _                        => -1
      ```
  entry .applyDynamicNamed   `Yaml.make(field = yaml, …)`: a mapping document from named arguments
      `Yaml.make(name = t"Anna".in[Yaml], age = 30.in[Yaml])` desugars to
      `applyDynamicNamed("make")(("name", …), ("age", …))` and builds a mapping with the
      entries in the order written; each argument is already a `Yaml`, so nest `Yaml.make`
      calls for nested mappings. The only method name accepted is `make`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      Yaml.make(name = t"Anna".in[Yaml], age = 30.in[Yaml]).as[Person]
      ```
  entry .showable   renders a document as block-style YAML text, given a `Yaml.Formatting`
      The `Showable` in `Yaml`'s companion, so `doc.show` works wherever a `Yaml.Formatting`
      is in scope (`import formatting.blockYamlFormatting`). The output is conservative
      block style, chosen to re-parse to a structurally identical tree: integers as bare
      digits, decimals through `Double.toString` or `.inf`, `-.inf`, `.nan`, booleans as
      `true`/`false`, null as `null`, a string plain only when it is a run of letters, digits,
      `_` and `-` beginning with a letter which does not spell a boolean or null, and
      double-quoted (with `\"`, `\\`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t` and `\uXXXX` escapes) otherwise;
      mappings and sequences are two-space-indented block collections, and empty ones `{}`
      and `[]`. The text always ends with a newline.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.blockYamlFormatting

      t"{name: Alice, tags: [x, y], meta: {}}".read[Yaml].show
      // name: Alice
      // tags:
      //   - x
      //   - y
      // meta: {}
      ```
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering of a document on one line: `yaml"name: Alice\nage: 30\n"`
      Needs no `Formatting`, so a debugger or a test failure can always show a document: the
      block-style text with its line breaks escaped, wrapped as `yaml"…"`, so a document is
      distinguishable from the `Text` which holds the same characters.
  entry .emit   renders a document a block at a time, pulled as an iterator or pushed to a function
      `Yaml.emit(doc)` is an `Iterator[Text]` which renders the next entries of the tree each
      time it is pulled, stopping between two entries once a block holds the `Buffering`
      capacity in scope (one long scalar may overrun it). `Yaml.emit(doc, deliver)` is the
      push form: it renders on the caller's thread and hands each block to `deliver` as it
      fills, the shape for writing to a file, socket or `OutputStream`. The blocks joined are
      exactly what `show` renders. Both need a `Yaml.Formatting` and a `Buffering`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.blockYamlFormatting

      val doc = t"items:\n  - 1\n  - nested:\n      deep: [1, 2]\n".read[Yaml]
      Yaml.emit(doc).to[List].join == doc.show
      ```
  entry .streamable   a document as a `Stream[Text] over Credit`, rendered as it is pulled
      Built on `.emit`, so the stream's chunks are the emitter's blocks; needs a
      `Yaml.Formatting` and a `Buffering`. This is what lets a `Yaml` be written to any
      text sink which takes a stream.
  entry .Formatting   the options a document is rendered under: currently only block style
      A `Yaml.Formatting` (a `zephyrine.Formatting`) must be in scope for `show`, `in[Text
      over Yaml]`, `.emit`, `.streamable`, `.abstractable` and the HTTP givens. YAML's block
      style is fixed and constrained by round-tripping, so the trait carries no options yet;
      `formatting.blockYamlFormatting` is the one instance, imported by name.
  entry .decodable   `Yaml is Decodable in Text`: `text.as[Yaml]` parses the whole text as a document
      As `Yaml is Decodable in Text`, this is `text.as[Yaml]`: it parses the whole text with
      `Yaml.Parser`, under the `PositionTracking` in scope (positions are recorded only with
      `parsing.trackPositions` imported), and raises `Parse.Error` for malformed input.
  entry .aggregable   parses a text stream as one document, as it arrives
      The `Yaml is Aggregable by Text` behind `text.read[Yaml]` and `stream.read[Yaml]`: a
      `Chain[Text]` is joined and parsed whole, but a `Stream[Text] over Credit` is
      transcoded to UTF-8 and fed to the parser's cursor as it is pulled, so a large document
      is never held whole and a `---` marker split across two chunks is still a boundary.
      Needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]` and honours the `PositionTracking` in scope.
  entry .aggregableData   parses a byte stream as one document, with no transcoding
      The `Yaml is Aggregable by Data` behind `data.read[Yaml]` and reading a file or an
      HTTP body: the parser is UTF-8-byte-based, so bytes are read as delivered, and a
      leading byte-order mark is absorbed. Needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`.
  entry .aggregableAll   reads a `---`-separated stream as a `List[Yaml]`, one per document
      `text.read[List[Yaml]]`: every document of a YAML stream, in order. Directives (`%YAML`,
      `%TAG`) apply only to the document which follows them, as the specification says, and
      an empty text gives an empty list. Honours `PositionTracking` as `.aggregable` does.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"---\n1\n---\n2\n---\n3".read[List[Yaml]].map(_.as[Int])   // List(1, 2, 3)
      ```
  entry .aggregableDerived   `import codecs.derived`: the tree path even where a direct parser is in scope
      Outranks `.aggregableParsed` only under `Codecs.Derived`, and sends the read through
      `.aggregableIn`.
  entry .aggregableStrict   `import codecs.strict`: the direct parser, or a compile error
      Under `Codecs.Strict`, a type with a `Yaml.Parsable` reads directly through this, and
      one without meets `.aggregableStrictFailure`, which reports _has no direct codec_ naming
      the type at the use site rather than falling back to the tree.
  entry .encodableOverDerived   `import codecs.derived`: the tree path even where a direct printer is in scope
  entry .encodableOverStrict   `import codecs.strict`: the direct printer, or a compile error
      Pairs with `.encodableOverStrictFailure`, which fails compilation for a type with no
      `Yaml.Printable` — for instance an enumeration, which has no product reflection and so
      no derived printer.
  entry .encodableOverData   `person.in[Data over Yaml]`: the YAML text as bytes in the `Codepage` in scope
      Encodes through `in[Text over Yaml]` and then the `Codepage` (such as
      `codepages.utf8Codepage`); `data.read[Person in Yaml]` reads it back.
  entry .abstractable   a document as an HTTP body, with the `application/yaml` content type
      `Yaml is Abstractable across HttpStreams to HttpStreams.Content`: the body is the
      rendered text in the `Codepage` in scope, with the content type `application/yaml;
      charset=<encoding>`. Needs a `Yaml.Formatting`; `ypsiloid.http`'s `postables` and
      `servables` givens are the telekinesis instances built from the same rendering.
  entry .instantiable   reads an HTTP request or response body back as a document
      `Yaml is Instantiable across HttpRequests from Text`: the body's text is parsed as
      `text.read[Yaml]`, raising `Parse.Error`, under the `PositionTracking` in scope.
  entry .yaml   `Yaml is Decodable in Yaml`: `doc.as[Yaml]` is the document itself
      The identity decoder, so a field typed `Yaml` in a case class keeps that part of the
      tree undecoded, to be inspected or decoded later.
  entry .yamlEncodable   `Yaml is Encodable in Yaml`: a document encodes as itself
      The identity encoder, the counterpart of `.yaml`: a `Yaml`-typed field is spliced into
      the mapping as it is, and `Yaml.make` takes its arguments through it.
  entry .int   `Int is Decodable in Yaml`: an integer or decimal scalar, truncated to `Int`
      A YAML integer (`42`, `0x2A`, `0o17`) or a decimal (truncated) reads as an `Int`; any
      other node registers `Yaml.Error` with `Reason.NotType(found, Integer)` — or
      `Reason.Absent` for a missing field — and continues with `0` so that sibling fields can
      accrue their own faults under a `validate` block. Under `throwUnsafely` the first fault
      throws.
  entry .long   `Long is Decodable in Yaml`: an integer or decimal scalar as a `Long`
      As `.int`. An integer beyond a `Long`'s range is held as a `Bcd` in the tree and
      truncates here; read it as `Yaml.Ast#bcd` through `Yaml.unseal` for its full precision.
  entry .double   `Double is Decodable in Yaml`: a decimal or integer scalar as a `Double`
      `.inf`, `-.inf` and `.nan` read as the IEEE values. A wrong-shaped node registers
      `Reason.NotType(found, Decimal)` and continues with `0.0`.
  entry .float   `Float is Decodable in Yaml`: a decimal or integer scalar as a `Float`
  entry .boolean   `Boolean is Decodable in Yaml`: a boolean scalar
      Only `true`/`false` (in any of YAML's spellings) resolve as booleans; a quoted `"true"`
      is a string and registers `Reason.NotType(Str, Bool)`.
  entry .text   `Text is Decodable in Yaml`: a string scalar
      A quoted scalar, a block scalar, a plain scalar which does not resolve as a number,
      boolean or null, or any scalar tagged `!!str`. A number is not coerced: `42` registers
      `Reason.NotType(Integer, Str)`; write `!!str 42` or `"42"` for the text.
  entry .string   `String is Decodable in Yaml`: a string scalar, as a Java `String`
  entry .unit   `Unit is Decodable in Yaml`: a null scalar
      `null`, `~`, an empty value or anything tagged `!!null`; any other node registers
      `Reason.NotType(found, Null)`.
  entry .bytes   `Bytes is Decodable in Yaml`: a byte count from an integer scalar
      Reads the node as a `Long` and makes a `Bytes` (hypotenuse's unit) of it.
  entry .listDecodable   `List[T] is Decodable in Yaml` for any element with a decoder
      Reads a sequence, focusing each element by its ordinal so an element's fault is
      reported at `#/items/2`. A node which is not a sequence registers
      `Reason.NotType(found, Sequence)`. `List` is Soundness's own (opaque) list, so this is
      a separate given from `.iterable`, which covers the stdlib collections.
  entry .setDecodable   `Set[T] is Decodable in Yaml`: a sequence read into a `Set`
  entry .seriesDecodable   `Sequence[T] is Decodable in Yaml`: a sequence read into a `Sequence`
  entry .iterable   any stdlib `Iterable` with a `Factory` is decodable from a sequence
      The general given for `scala.collection` types (`Vector`, `scala.List`, …) built
      through their `Factory`; `.listDecodable`, `.setDecodable` and `.seriesDecodable` are
      its instances for Soundness's own collections.
  entry .map   `Map[Text, T] is Decodable in Yaml`: a mapping read with its keys as text
      Each key must be a scalar: a string is taken as is, a number or boolean key is
      rendered as text, and a `null` key becomes `t"null"`; a collection key aborts with
      `Reason.NotType(found, Str)`. A node which is not a mapping registers
      `Reason.NotType(found, Mapping)` and continues with an empty map.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"a: 1\nb: 2".read[Yaml].as[Map[Text, Int]]   // Map(t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2)
      ```
  entry .option   `Option[T] is Decodable in Yaml`, under the `optionalityOptions` policies
      A missing key reads as `None` unless `strictYamlAbsence` is in scope (then
      `Yaml.Error(Absent)`); a YAML `null` reads as `None` unless `strictYamlNulls` is in
      scope (then the inner decoder sees it, and fails for anything but `Unit`); and a value
      the inner decoder rejects is an error unless `lenientYamlFaults` is in scope (then
      `None`). The three policies are `Decodable.Absence`, `Nullity` and `Fault` `in Yaml`,
      with the lenient-absence, lenient-null, strict-fault defaults in distillate.
  entry .optionEncodable   `Option[T] is Encodable in Yaml`: `None` encodes as absent
  entry .intEncodable   `Int is Encodable in Yaml`: an integer scalar
  entry .longEncodable   `Long is Encodable in Yaml`: an integer scalar
  entry .integralEncodable   any `Integral` type encodes as an integer scalar, through `toLong`
  entry .doubleEncodable   `Double is Encodable in Yaml`: a decimal scalar, with `.inf` and `.nan` spelt as YAML does
  entry .floatEncodable   `Float is Encodable in Yaml`: a decimal scalar
  entry .booleanEncodable   `Boolean is Encodable in Yaml`: `true` or `false`
  entry .textEncodable   `Text is Encodable in Yaml`: a string scalar, quoted when rendering requires
      The tree holds the text as it is; `show` decides whether it can be a plain scalar or
      must be double-quoted, so `t"42"` round-trips as the string `"42"` and not the number.
  entry .stringEncodable   `String is Encodable in Yaml`: a string scalar
  entry .unitEncodable   `Unit is Encodable in Yaml`: a `null` scalar
  entry .listEncodable   `List[T] is Encodable in Yaml`: a sequence of the elements' encodings
  entry .setAliasEncodable   `Set[T] is Encodable in Yaml`: a sequence, in the set's iteration order
  entry .seriesAliasEncodable   `Sequence[T] is Encodable in Yaml`: a sequence
  entry .iterableEncodable   any stdlib `Iterable` encodes as a sequence
  entry .mapEncodable   `Map[K, V] is Encodable in Yaml` for any key with an `Encodable in Text`
      Keys are rendered to text through their `Encodable in Text` and become string scalars;
      values encode through their `Encodable in Yaml`. The entries follow the map's iteration
      order.
  entry .discriminatedUnion   a `Discriminable in Yaml` keyed on a mapping field of any name
      `Yaml.discriminatedUnion[Shape](t"kind")` is how a sum type's variant is named on the
      wire: `discriminate` reads the string under `kind`, `rewrite` adds or replaces the field
      with the variant's name when encoding, and `variant` strips it before the variant's own
      decoder runs. The `discriminables` package holds the two common choices, `type` and
      `kind`, as importable givens; call this for any other field name and bind the result as
      a local given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      enum Shape:
        case Circle(radius: Double)
        case Square(side: Double)

      given Shape is Discriminable in Yaml = Yaml.discriminatedUnion[Shape](t"shape")
      t"{shape: Circle, radius: 2.5}".read[Yaml].as[Shape]   // Shape.Circle(2.5)
      ```
  entry .positionable   `doc.locate(path)` and `doc.locateKey(path)`: a node's source position
      The `Yaml is Positionable by YamlPath to Yaml.Ast.Position` behind zephyrine's `locate`
      extensions. `locate` gives the position of the value at a `YamlPath` and `locateKey`
      that of the mapping key which holds it; both are `Unset` for an untracked document
      (parsed without `parsing.trackPositions`), for a path which does not resolve, and
      `locateKey` for the root. Lines and columns are 1-based.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import parsing.trackPositions

      val doc = t"outer:\n  inner: 99\n".read[Yaml]
      doc.locate(YamlPath()(t"outer")(t"inner"))      // line 2, column 10, length 2
      doc.locateKey(YamlPath()(t"outer")(t"inner"))   // line 2, column 3, length 5
      ```
  entry .interpolator   the given behind `y"…"`: a YAML literal parsed as the code compiles
      `Yaml is Interpolable`, with `Yaml` as its result. See `ypsiloid.y` for what the
      interpolator accepts.
  entry .extrapolator   the given behind `case y"…" =>`: a YAML literal as a pattern
      `Yaml is Extrapolable`: a `y"…"` pattern matches a document structurally, and a `$x`
      hole in it binds the node at that position as a `Yaml`. See `ypsiloid.y`.
  entry .lens   `"field" is Lens from Yaml onto Yaml`: a mapping entry as a lens, by its label
      The given which makes `doc.lens(_.inner.n = value)` work: each label in the path is a
      lens whose getter is `#selectDynamic` and whose setter replaces (or inserts) the entry,
      so a nested update rebuilds the mapping at each level and returns a new document.
      It is gated on a `Yaml is Dynamical` (`dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml`) and needs a
      `Tactic[Yaml.Error]`. The assigned value must be a `Yaml`: encode it with `in[Yaml]`,
      or import `conversions.encodableToYaml` to assign any encodable value directly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml
      import conversions.encodableToYaml

      case class Inner(n: Int)
      case class Outer(name: Text, inner: Inner)

      val doc = Outer(t"a", Inner(7)).in[Yaml]
      doc.lens(_.inner.n = 99).as[Outer]            // Outer(t"a", Inner(99))
      doc.lens(_.extra = 7)(t"extra").as[Int]       // 7: an absent field is inserted
      ```
  entry .ordinalOptical   `Ordinal is Optical from Yaml onto Yaml`: one sequence item, by position
      Makes `doc.lens(_.items(Prim) = value)` update the first item of a sequence-valued
      field; an ordinal past the end, or a node which is not a sequence, leaves the document
      unchanged.
  entry .eachOptical   `Each is Optical from Yaml onto Yaml`: every item of a sequence
      `doc.lens(_.items(Each) = 0)` applies the update to every item of the sequence, and is
      a no-op on anything which is not one.
  entry .filterOptical   `Filter[Yaml] is Optical from Yaml onto Yaml`: the items a predicate selects
      `doc.lens(_.items(Filter[Yaml](_.as[Int] > 15)) = 0)` updates only the items for which
      the predicate holds and leaves the rest as they are.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml
      import conversions.encodableToYaml

      val doc = t"items: [10, 20, 30]".read[Yaml]
      doc.lens(_.items(Filter[Yaml](_.as[Int] > 15)) = 0).items.as[List[Int]]   // List(10, 0, 0)
      ```
  entry .primitive   the `Yaml.Primitive` kind of a node: what an error reports as _found_
      `Yaml.primitive(ast)` classifies a node as `Null`, `Bool`, `Integer`, `Decimal`, `Str`,
      `Sequence` or `Mapping`; a high-precision `Bcd` number reports as `Decimal`, and the
      absent node as `Null`.
  entry .YamlString   the JVM representation of a string scalar in the tree: `String`
  entry .YamlInteger   the representation of an integer scalar: `Long`, or `Bcd` beyond a `Long`'s range
  entry .YamlDecimal   the representation of a decimal scalar: `Double`, or `Bcd` beyond 17 significant digits
  entry .YamlBoolean   the representation of a boolean scalar: `Boolean`
  entry .YamlNull   the sentinel for a YAML `null` in the tree, distinct from the absent node
      The object and its singleton type. A YAML `null` and an absent value (a missing field,
      represented as `Unset`) must stay distinct — an `Optional` field treats them under
      different policies — so the null is this sentinel rather than the JVM's `null`.
      `Yaml.Ast.Null` is the node, and `isNull` the test.
  entry .YamlSequence   the representation of a sequence: a frozen `Array[Any]` of odd length
      Sequences and mappings share one array representation and are told apart by length
      parity: a sequence array is always odd-length, padded with a sentinel when the item
      count is even. `Yaml.Ast.sequenceLength` gives the real count.
  entry .YamlMapping   the representation of a mapping: a frozen `Array[Any]` of alternating keys and values
      Always even-length, `[k0, v0, k1, v1, …]`, in document order. Keys are nodes, and only
      string keys are addressable by `Yaml#apply`, `YamlPath` and the derived decoders.
  entry .PositionIndex   the flat index of source positions a tracked parse records beside the tree
      An opaque frozen `Array[Int]` of position descriptors, one per node, laid out so that
      the slice at any descriptor boundary is itself a valid index. It is built only when
      `parsing.trackPositions` is in scope, carried as `Yaml#positionIndex`, and walked by
      `.positionable` to answer `locate` and `locateKey`; there is no public way to read it
      directly.
  entry .Focus   where a decoding fault happened: its `YamlPath`, and its position if the document was tracked
    detail
        The focus `Yaml#as` tracks, so that an accruing strategy can report each fault at its
        place: `pointer` is the path to the field (`#/address/city`, root-first), and
        `position` is its line and column, filled in by `#withPosition` for every registered
        focus when the document has a `positionIndex`, and `Unset` otherwise.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import parsing.trackPositions

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int, email: Text)

        case class Issues(items: List[Text] = Nil)(using Diagnostics)
        extends Error(m"${items.size} issues"):
          def +(focus: Text): Issues = Issues(items :+ focus)

        val doc = t"name: Alice\nage: thirty\n".read[Yaml]

        validate[Yaml.Focus](Issues()):
          case error: Yaml.Error => accrual + prior.let(_.pointer.encode).or(t"#")
        . protect(doc.as[Person]).items   // List(t"#/age", t"#/email")
        ```
    entry #pointer   the path of the faulting node, root-first
    entry #position   the node's source position, or `Unset` for an untracked document
    entry #withPosition   a copy with `position` resolved against a document's index
        `focus.withPosition(doc)` is `focus` with `position` set to `doc.locate(pointer)`;
        `Yaml#as` applies it to every registered focus, so a handler rarely needs to.
  entry .Ast   a node of the tree: an unboxed scalar, a sequence or a mapping, inspected through extensions
    detail
        `Yaml.Ast` is an opaque type over the JVM representations of YAML's values — a
        `String`, `Long`, `Double`, `Boolean` or `Bcd` scalar, the `YamlNull` sentinel, a
        frozen array for a sequence or mapping, and `Unset` for an absent value — so a scalar
        is stored without a wrapper. The companion is the whole API: constructors (`Str`,
        `Integer`, `Sequence`, …) which double as extractors for pattern matching,
        predicates (`isString`, `isObject`, …) and accessors (`string`, `arrayElement`,
        `objectIndexOf`, …) as extension methods, `deepEquals` for structural comparison,
        and the `Position` and `Issue` types a parse reports.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val node: Yaml.Ast = Yaml.unseal(t"{name: Alice, tags: [a, b]}".read[Yaml])

        node.isObject                                    // true
        node.objectIndexOf("tags")                       // 1
        node.objectValue(1).arrayLength                  // 2
        node.objectValue(1).arrayElement(0).string       // t"a"

        node match
          case Yaml.Ast.Mapping(entries) => entries.length
          case _                         => 0
        ```

        A document (`Yaml`) wraps a node; most code works through the document and its
        codecs, and reaches the node only to match on structure or to read a high-precision
        number as a `Bcd`. The companion is also the `Format` of a YAML parse, named
        `YAML`, whose `Parse.Error`s carry a `Yaml.Ast.Issue` and a `Yaml.Ast.Position`.
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Ast.Issue
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Ast.Position
    entry .name   the format's name, `YAML`, as `Parse.Error` messages report it
    entry .showable   renders a node as block-style YAML text, given a `Yaml.Formatting`
        The same emitter as `Yaml`'s `showable`, on a bare node.
    entry .inspectable   a debug rendering of a node on one line, suffixed `ᵃˢᵗ`
        As a document inspects, with its line breaks escaped, plus a superscript `ᵃˢᵗ` so a
        bare node is distinguishable from the `Yaml` which would wrap it.
    entry .apply   wraps any representation value as a node
        `Yaml.Ast(value)` for a `Long`, `Double`, `Bcd`, `Boolean`, `String`, frozen
        `Array[Any]`, `YamlNull` or `Unset`; the typed constructors `.Str`, `.Integer`, … are
        clearer where the kind is known.
    entry .Null   the `null` node
    entry .Bool   `Yaml.Ast.Bool(true)` makes a boolean node; `case Yaml.Ast.Bool(b)` matches one
    entry .Integer   `Yaml.Ast.Integer(42L)` makes an integer node; `case Yaml.Ast.Integer(n)` matches one
        The extractor matches only a `Long`-ranged integer; an integer held as a `Bcd`
        matches `.BcdValue`.
    entry .Decimal   `Yaml.Ast.Decimal(3.14)` makes a decimal node; `case Yaml.Ast.Decimal(d)` matches one
    entry .BcdValue   a high-precision number node, held as a `Bcd`; matches one in a pattern
        An integer beyond a `Long`'s range, or a decimal with more than 17 significant
        digits, parses to a `Bcd` rather than being rounded; this constructs or extracts one.
    entry .Str   `Yaml.Ast.Str(t"x")` makes a string node; `case Yaml.Ast.Str(text)` matches one
    entry .Sequence   `Yaml.Ast.Sequence(items)` makes a sequence from a frozen array of nodes; matches one
        The extractor yields the items as a frozen `Array[Yaml.Ast]`, without the padding
        sentinel the representation may carry; both the constructor and the extractor copy
        when the count is even.
    entry .Mapping   `Yaml.Ast.Mapping(entries)` makes a mapping from key-value pairs; matches one
        The constructor takes a frozen `Array[(Yaml.Ast, Yaml.Ast)]` and flattens it; the
        extractor yields the pairs, in document order.
    entry .sequenceLength   the item count of a sequence array, excluding the padding sentinel
        A low-level accessor on the representation (`Array[Any]`); `arrayLength` is the
        same on a node.
    entry .mappingSize   the entry count of a mapping array: half its length
    entry .foreachItem   applies an inline body to each item of a sequence array
        A hot-path loop for collection decoders, with no iterator and no boxing of the index.
    entry .foreachEntry   applies an inline body to each key and value of a mapping array
    entry .deepEquals   structural equality of two nodes, walking sequences and mappings
        Two parses of the same text are different array instances; this compares them by
        content, and is what `Yaml#equals` uses. Numbers compare by kind and value, so
        `Integer(1)` and `Decimal(1.0)` are not equal.
    entry .deepHash   a hash consistent with `.deepEquals`, used by `Yaml#hashCode`
    entry .isAbsent   whether the node is the absent sentinel: a missing field, or `Yaml#apply` on a missing key
    entry .isNull   whether the node is a YAML `null`
    entry .isLong   whether the node is an integer within a `Long`'s range
    entry .isDouble   whether the node is a decimal held as a `Double`
    entry .isBcd   whether the node is a high-precision number held as a `Bcd`
    entry .isNumber   whether the node is an integer or decimal of any precision
    entry .isString   whether the node is a string scalar
    entry .isBoolean   whether the node is a boolean scalar
    entry .isObject   whether the node is a mapping
    entry .isArray   whether the node is a sequence
    entry .arrayLength   the item count of a sequence node; undefined on any other node
        The accessors `arrayLength`, `arrayElement`, `objectSize`, `objectKey` and
        `objectValue` are unchecked for speed: test `isArray` or `isObject` first, or use the
        checked `array`, `string`, `long`, … which register a `Yaml.Error`.
    entry .arrayElement   the item at an index of a sequence node, unchecked
    entry .objectSize   the entry count of a mapping node, unchecked
    entry .objectKey   the key at a pair index of a mapping node, as a `String`, unchecked
        The key's `String`; a non-string key fails the cast, so use `objectIndexOf` or match
        on `.Mapping` for a mapping which may have scalar keys of other kinds.
    entry .objectValue   the value at a pair index of a mapping node, unchecked
    entry .objectIndexOf   the pair index of a string key in a mapping, or `-1`
        A linear scan matching only string keys; `-1` for a missing key and for a node which
        is not a mapping. The index feeds `objectKey` and `objectValue`.
    entry .array   the items of a sequence node, or a registered `Yaml.Error` and an empty array
        Needs a `Tactic[Yaml.Error]`: a node which is not a sequence registers
        `Reason.NotType(found, Sequence)` (or `Reason.Absent`) and continues with no items.
    entry .double   the node as a `Double`, from a decimal, integer or `Bcd`; else a registered `Yaml.Error`
    entry .long   the node as a `Long`, from an integer, decimal or `Bcd`; else a registered `Yaml.Error`
    entry .bcd   the node as a `Bcd`, at full precision, from any number; else a registered `Yaml.Error`
        The way to read a number too large for a `Long` or too precise for a `Double`
        without loss: `Yaml.unseal(doc).bcd.toBigDecimal`.
    entry .string   the node as `Text`, from a string scalar; else a registered `Yaml.Error`
    entry .boolean   the node as a `Boolean`; else a registered `Yaml.Error`
    entry .primitive   the node's `Yaml.Primitive` kind
    entry .Position   a source position: line and column (1-based), with the node's offset and length
      detail
          The `Format.Position` of a YAML parse, carried by every `Parse.Error` and returned
          by `locate`. `line` and `column` are 1-based as an editor shows them; `span` is the
          0-based range a diagnostic highlights. `describe` renders `line 2, column 10`.
      entry #line   the 1-based line
      entry #column   the 1-based column
      entry #offset   the character offset from the start of the source, if known
      entry #length   the length of the node's source text, if known
      entry #describe   the position as text: `line 2, column 10`
      entry #span   the position as a 0-based `Span` of `length` characters
    entry .Issue   what a parse can find wrong, each with its message
      detail
          The `Format.Issue` of a YAML parse: a `Parse.Error`'s `issue` is one of these, and
          `describe` gives its message. The cases cover the document structure (a `---`
          missing between documents, content after `...`, a directive out of place or
          malformed, `%YAML` repeated or without a `major.minor` version, `%TAG` without a
          handle and prefix), node properties (two anchors or two tags on a node, an anchor on
          an alias, an unterminated verbatim tag, `UndefinedTagHandle(handle)`,
          `UnknownAlias(name)`, a reserved indicator at a node's start), block structure (a
          tab in indentation, a mapping starting on the `---` line, a sequence on a key's
          line, a key without its `:`, a multi-line implicit key, a chained value on one
          line, under-indented continuation), quoted scalars (unterminated single or double
          quotes, an unterminated, invalid or truncated escape, a bad hex digit, trailing
          content after the closing quote, a document marker inside a multi-line scalar),
          flow collections (unterminated `[` or `{`, an empty entry, `,` or the closer
          expected, a key and its `:` on different lines, under-indented content, a document
          marker in flow context), block scalars (a header which is invalid, an indentation
          indicator outside 1–9 or repeated, a repeated chomping indicator, leading blank
          lines over-indented, a header comment without whitespace before it), and a comment
          not preceded by whitespace.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          capture[Parse.Error](t"a: *missing".read[Yaml]).issue
          // Yaml.Ast.Issue.UnknownAlias(t"missing")
          ```
      entry #describe   the issue's message, such as _the alias *name does not refer to a known anchor_
    entry .Byte   the ASCII byte constants the parser switches on
      hidden
      detail
          Not for direct use: named constants (`Colon`, `Minus`, `OpenBracket`, …) for the
          byte values the parser's dispatch tables compare against.
  entry .Error   raised when a document cannot be decoded as the type asked for
    detail
        Error 31: _could not access the YAML value because `reason`_. Raised by `Yaml#as`,
        `Yaml#apply`, the updates and every `Decodable in Yaml` when a node has the wrong
        shape or a required field is missing. The reason is one of two:
        `Reason.NotType(found, expected)` — _the YAML value had type `found` instead of
        `expected`_, both `Yaml.Primitive`s — and `Reason.Absent` — _the YAML value was not
        present_. The primitive decoders register the error and continue with a sentinel,
        so under an accruing strategy a record with three bad fields reports three errors,
        each focused on its field; the gated construction never runs the record's
        constructor on sentinel values.

        A parse failure is not a `Yaml.Error`: malformed text raises zephyrine's
        `Parse.Error`, with a `Yaml.Ast.Issue` and a `Yaml.Ast.Position`.
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Focus
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Primitive
    entry #reason   why the value could not be read: `NotType(found, expected)` or `Absent`
    entry .Reason   the two reasons: a node of the wrong kind, or a missing value
      detail
          `NotType(found: Yaml.Primitive, expected: Yaml.Primitive)` (number 1) is a node of
          the wrong kind, such as a string where an integer was due; `Absent` (number 2) is a
          field the mapping does not have, or a `null` or missing value under a strict
          optionality policy. Both are `Clarification`s, so an error's number is `31.1` or
          `31.2`.
      entry .communicable   renders a reason as its message
  entry .Primitive   the seven kinds of node, as an error names what it found and expected
    detail
        `Null`, `Bool`, `Integer`, `Decimal`, `Str`, `Sequence` and `Mapping`.
        `Yaml.primitive(node)` classifies a node; a `Bcd` number is `Decimal`, and the absent
        node is `Null`.
    entry .communicable   renders a primitive's name in a message
  entry .Direct   the `derives` carrier for a record's direct parser and printer
    detail
        `case class Config(…) derives Yaml.Direct` derives `Config`'s `Yaml.Parsable` and
        `Yaml.Printable` once, into its companion, where they serve every `read[Config in
        Yaml]` and `in[Text over Yaml]` — with or without `import codecs.direct`, which would
        otherwise derive them afresh at each use, and ahead of it, since a companion instance
        outranks the import. A `Self`-typed typeclass cannot appear in a `derives` clause
        itself, which is why this parameterised carrier exists; `.declared` in `Parsable`
        and `Printable` unpack it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import formatting.blockYamlFormatting

        case class Config(port: Int, host: Text) derives Yaml.Direct

        Config(8080, t"localhost").in[Text over Yaml].read[Config in Yaml]
        ```
    entry .derived   derives a `Direct` for a product: its parser and printer, field by field
    entry #parsable   the record's direct parser
    entry #printable   the record's direct printer
  entry .Parsable   a type read straight off the parsed tree, with no `Yaml` built around each node
    detail
        The opt-in surface of direct reading: a type with a `Yaml.Parsable` is read by
        `read[T in Yaml]` through `.aggregableParsed` rather than through a `Decodable in
        Yaml`. An instance comes from `derives Yaml.Direct`, from `Parsable.derived[T]` bound
        as a given, from `import codecs.direct` (every product, derived at the use site), or
        from the bridges `fromField` and `fromDecodable`. A `Parsable` is a `Yaml.Field`, so
        a record's parser also serves as a field parser inside another record. A direct
        parser gives the same results and the same focused errors as the tree path, and
        honours `@name`, defaults and the `optionalityOptions` policies.
        The `generated` given is inherited from the priority layer `Parsable2`, and
        documented there.
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Field
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Direct
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Reader
    entry .derived   derives a direct parser for a product from its `ProductReflection`
    entry .fromField   a `Parsable` from a `Yaml.Field`: the opt-in surface from the always-resolving one
    entry .fromDecodable   a `Parsable` which decodes the node as a `Yaml` through a `Decodable in Yaml`
        The bridge from the tree world, whole: for a type whose reading cannot be direct
        but which must satisfy `codecs.strict`.
    entry .declared   the parser of a type which `derives Yaml.Direct`, from its companion's `Direct`
  entry .Field   a value read from a node of the tree, as a derived parser reads each field
    detail
        The always-resolving supertype of `Yaml.Parsable`: every type has a `Yaml.Field` —
        the companion's givens for the primitives, `Text`, `Unit`, `Option`, `Optional`,
        `List`, `Set`, `Sequence`, `Map[Text, _]` and any stdlib `Iterable`, a derived one
        for any product (`.product`), and a bridge from any `Decodable in Yaml`
        (`.decodable`) — so a derived parser can read any field type. A `Field` reads from
        `(reader, node)`, where the node is the absent sentinel for a key the mapping lacks.
        The `product` and `decodable` givens are inherited from the priority layers `Field1`
        and `Field2`, and documented there.
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Parsable
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Reader
    entry #Transport   the format a field is read from: `Yaml`
    entry #Reader   the reader a field reads through: `Yaml.Reader`
    entry #parse   reads the value from a node, or from the absent node when called with the reader alone
    entry .parseField   reads one field through its `Field`, from the reader carried as an `AnyRef`
        A rim call point for derived code, which cannot hold the capability-typed reader in
        a function parameter.
    entry .readerOf   the reader, from its `AnyRef` carrier
    entry .lookup   the value under a string key in a mapping node, or `null`
        `null` also for a node which is not a mapping. Only string keys match, as the tree
        decoder's lookup.
    entry .int   `Int is Yaml.Field`: an integer or decimal node
    entry .long   `Long is Yaml.Field`
    entry .double   `Double is Yaml.Field`
    entry .float   `Float is Yaml.Field`
    entry .boolean   `Boolean is Yaml.Field`
    entry .text   `Text is Yaml.Field`: a string node
    entry .string   `String is Yaml.Field`
    entry .unit   `Unit is Yaml.Field`: a null node
    entry .optional   `Optional[T] is Yaml.Field`, under the `optionalityOptions` policies
        As the tree decoder's `Yaml.optional`: absent and `null` read as `Unset` unless
        `strictYamlAbsence` or `strictYamlNulls` is in scope, and a rejected value is a fault
        unless `lenientYamlFaults` is.
    entry .option   `Option[T] is Yaml.Field`, under the same policies, yielding `None`
    entry .iterable   any stdlib `Iterable` with a `Factory`, from a sequence node, each item focused by ordinal
    entry .list   `List[T] is Yaml.Field`: a sequence node
    entry .set   `Set[T] is Yaml.Field`: a sequence node
    entry .series   `Sequence[T] is Yaml.Field`: a sequence node
    entry .map   `Map[Text, T] is Yaml.Field`: a mapping node with string keys
  entry .Reader   the context a direct parser reads under: the tactic and foci of the read
    detail
        A `Yaml.Parsable` reads its nodes through a `Reader`, which holds the
        `Tactic[Yaml.Error]` and `Foci[Yaml.Focus]` of the read, so instances are
        construction-free and the same parser serves every read. Its scalar methods register
        a wrong-kind or absent fault through the tactic and continue with a sentinel, as the
        tree decoders do, so sibling fields accrue their own errors. Only
        `.aggregableParsed` makes one; a hand-written `Parsable` receives it.
    entry #int   an integer or decimal node as an `Int`, or a registered fault and `0`
    entry #long   an integer or decimal node as a `Long`, or a registered fault and `0`
    entry #double   a decimal or integer node as a `Double`, or a registered fault and `0.0`
    entry #float   a decimal or integer node as a `Float`, or a registered fault and `0.0f`
    entry #boolean   a boolean node, or a registered fault and `false`
    entry #string   a string node as a `String`, or a registered fault and `""`
    entry #unit   checks the node is `null`, registering a fault otherwise
    entry #shape   registers a node of the wrong shape — a scalar where a sequence or mapping was due — and continues
  entry .Printable   a type written straight to YAML text from its fields, with no tree built
    detail
        The opt-in surface of direct writing: a type with a `Yaml.Printable` is written by
        `in[Text over Yaml]` through `.encodableOverPrinted`, and by `Printable.print` and
        `Printable.emit`, rather than through an `Encodable in Yaml` and `show`. An instance
        comes from `derives Yaml.Direct`, from `Printable.derived[T]`, from `import
        codecs.direct`, or from the bridges `fromWritable` and `fromEncodable`. The text is
        exactly what `show` renders for the value's tree.
        The `generated` given is inherited from the priority layer `Printable2`, and
        documented there.
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Writable
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Direct
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Out
    entry .derived   derives a direct printer for a product from its `ProductReflection`
    entry .fromWritable   a `Printable` from a `Yaml.Writable`
    entry .fromEncodable   a `Printable` which encodes the value to a tree and writes that in place
    entry .declared   the printer of a type which `derives Yaml.Direct`, from its companion's `Direct`
    entry .print   a value's whole YAML text through its `Printable`
        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int) derives Yaml.Direct

        Yaml.Printable.print(Person(t"Ada", 36))   // name: Ada\nage: 36\n
        ```
    entry .emit   writes a value's text in blocks of the `Buffering` capacity, handing each to a function
        The push form for a large value: each completed line is handed to `deliver` once the
        text reaches the capacity, so no text of the whole value is ever built.
  entry .Writable   a value written as a mapping value, a sequence item or a document, as a derived printer writes each field
    detail
        The always-resolving supertype of `Yaml.Printable`: every type has a `Yaml.Writable`
        — the companion's givens for the primitives, `Text`, `Unit`, `Option`, `Optional`,
        `List`, `Set`, `Sequence`, `Map` and any stdlib `Iterable`, a derived one for any
        product (`.product`), and a bridge from any `Encodable in Yaml` (`.encodable`). A
        `Writable` writes into a `Yaml.Out` as the value due after a `key:` or `-` marker,
        and says through `#absent` whether it should be left out of a mapping altogether.
        The `product` and `encodable` givens are inherited from the priority layers
        `Writable1` and `Writable2`, and documented there.
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Printable
    refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Out
    entry #absent   whether a value is left out of a mapping: an `Unset` or `None` field
    entry #write   writes the value into a `Yaml.Out`, as the value due after a marker or as the document
    entry .writeField   writes one field through its `Writable`, into the writer carried as an `AnyRef`
    entry .keyOf   writes a `key:` marker into the writer carried as an `AnyRef`
    entry .int   `Int is Yaml.Writable`
    entry .long   `Long is Yaml.Writable`
    entry .double   `Double is Yaml.Writable`
    entry .float   `Float is Yaml.Writable`
    entry .boolean   `Boolean is Yaml.Writable`
    entry .text   `Text is Yaml.Writable`: plain or double-quoted, as `show` decides
    entry .string   `String is Yaml.Writable`
    entry .unit   `Unit is Yaml.Writable`: writes `null`
    entry .optional   `Optional[T] is Yaml.Writable`: `Unset` is absent, and omitted from a mapping
    entry .option   `Option[T] is Yaml.Writable`: `None` is absent
    entry .iterable   any stdlib `Iterable` writes as a block sequence, `[]` when empty
    entry .list   `List[T] is Yaml.Writable`: a block sequence
    entry .set   `Set[T] is Yaml.Writable`: a block sequence
    entry .series   `Sequence[T] is Yaml.Writable`: a block sequence
    entry .map   `Map[K, V] is Yaml.Writable` for any key with an `Encodable in Text`: a block mapping, `{}` when empty
  entry .Out   the writer a direct printer writes into: text so far, indentation, and the marker awaiting its value
    detail
        Lays text out exactly as the block emitter lays out a tree: `#key` or `#item` writes
        a `key:` or `-` marker and leaves the value due after it; a scalar then writes on the
        same line, and a non-empty collection on the lines below, one step deeper, between
        `#open` and `#close`. Each completed line is handed on once the text reaches the
        writer's limit, so `Printable.emit` can write a value a block at a time. Only
        `Printable.print` and `Printable.emit` make one; a hand-written `Writable` receives
        it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Point(x: Int, y: Int)

        given Point is Yaml.Printable:
          def write(point: Point, out: Yaml.Out^): Unit =
            val outer = out.open()
            out.key("x"); out.long(point.x)
            out.key("y"); out.long(point.y)
            out.close(outer)

        Yaml.Printable.print(Point(1, 2))   // x: 1\ny: 2\n
        ```
    entry #long   writes an integer as the value due
    entry #double   writes a decimal as the value due, with `.inf`, `-.inf` and `.nan` as YAML spells them
    entry #boolean   writes `true` or `false` as the value due
    entry #string   writes a string as the value due, plain or double-quoted as `show` decides
    entry #nullValue   writes `null` as the value due
    entry #empty   writes `{}` or `[]` as the value due
    entry #key   writes the `key:` marker of a mapping entry, at the current indentation
    entry #item   writes the `-` marker of a sequence item
    entry #open   opens a non-empty collection on the lines below its marker, returning what `#close` restores
        At the top of the document nothing is due, and the entries write at the margin.
    entry #close   restores the indentation `#open` returned
    entry #tree   writes a `Yaml.Ast` as the value due, as the block emitter would
  entry .FieldDerivation   the derivation of a product's `Yaml.Field`, behind direct parsing
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: `Yaml.Field.product` and `Yaml.Parsable.derived` reach it.
    entry .conjunction   a product's field parser: each field read off the mapping's nodes
    entry .FieldProductParser   the `Field` a derived parser becomes, over a lambda taking the reader as an `AnyRef`
  entry .WritableDerivation   the derivation of a product's `Yaml.Writable`, behind direct printing
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: `Yaml.Writable.product` and `Yaml.Printable.derived` reach it.
    entry .conjunction   a product's field writer: each present field written after its `key:` marker
    entry .WritableProductPrinter   the `Writable` a derived printer becomes, over a lambda taking the writer as an `AnyRef`
  entry .Field1   the priority layer holding `Yaml.Field.product`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: a parent of `object Yaml.Field`, so that the derived product
        parser ranks below the companion's own givens and above the `Decodable` bridge.
    entry #product   a derived field parser for any product, from its `ProductReflection`
        Inline, in `Yaml.Field1`, so a nested record inside a direct parser is itself read
        directly, with no codec dispatched for it.
  entry .Field2   the priority layer holding `Yaml.Field.decodable`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: the lowest parent of `object Yaml.Field`.
    entry #decodable   the bridge: any `Decodable in Yaml` reads the node wrapped as a `Yaml`
        In `Yaml.Field2`, lowest in priority, so a type with no direct field parser of its
        own — an enumeration, or a type with a hand-written decoder — still reads inside a
        direct parser, through the tree path for that one field.
  entry .Writable1   the priority layer holding `Yaml.Writable.product`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: a parent of `object Yaml.Writable`.
    entry #product   a derived field writer for any product, in `Yaml.Writable1`
  entry .Writable2   the priority layer holding `Yaml.Writable.encodable`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: the lowest parent of `object Yaml.Writable`.
    entry #encodable   the bridge: any `Encodable in Yaml` writes its tree in place, in `Yaml.Writable2`
  entry .Parsable2   the priority layer holding `Yaml.Parsable.generated`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: the parent of `object Yaml.Parsable`, so that `import
        codecs.direct` ranks below a companion instance or `derives Yaml.Direct`.
    entry #generated   `import codecs.direct`: a derived parser for every product, at the use site
        Resolves only under `Codecs.Direct`, and only for a type with a
        `ProductReflection`; an enumeration keeps the tree path. A companion instance or a
        `derives Yaml.Direct` outranks it.
  entry .Printable2   the priority layer holding `Yaml.Printable.generated`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: the parent of `object Yaml.Printable`.
    entry #generated   `import codecs.direct`: a derived printer for every product, at the use site

entry .Yaml1   the priority layer of `object Yaml` holding the direct-codec entry points
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: a parent of `object Yaml`, holding `aggregableParsed`,
      `encodableOverPrinted` and the two `codecs.strict` failures, so that a type with a
      direct codec takes it ahead of the tree codecs in `Yaml2`.
  entry #aggregableParsed   `text.read[Person in Yaml]` through a direct parser, where one exists
      For a type with a `Yaml.Parsable` — one which `derives Yaml.Direct`, has an instance
      in its companion, or is a product under `import codecs.direct` — the document is still
      parsed whole (YAML's aliases can refer back to any earlier node, so no record can be
      read before the document is held), but the record's fields are then read straight off
      the tree's nodes with no `Yaml` built around any of them and no codec dispatched per
      field. Errors carry the same focus and position as the tree path's.
  entry #encodableOverPrinted   `in[Text over Yaml]` through a direct printer, where one exists
      For a type with a `Yaml.Printable`, the text is written straight from the fields, with
      no tree built, through `Yaml.Printable.print`; the text is the same as the tree path's.
  entry #aggregableStrictFailure   the compile error `codecs.strict` raises for a type with no direct parser
      Inline and above the tree entries, so under `Codecs.Strict` any type `.aggregableStrict`
      does not take reaches it and fails to compile, naming the type.
  entry #encodableOverStrictFailure   the compile error `codecs.strict` raises for a type with no direct printer

entry .Yaml2   the lowest priority layer of `object Yaml`: the tree codecs and the derivations
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the lowest parent of `object Yaml`, holding the fallback
      `decodable` and `encodable` givens for every type, `optional`, `optionalEncodable`,
      `encodableOver`, `aggregableIn` and the two derivation objects.
  entry #decodable   any type's `Decodable in Yaml`, from its text decoder or by derivation
      As `[value] => value is Decodable in Yaml`, inline and lowest in priority, it gives
      every type a decoder: a type with a `Decodable in Text` is read from a YAML string
      scalar (so an enumeration with a text decoder reads from its name), and a type with a
      wisteria `Reflection` — a case class or a sealed hierarchy — is derived by
      `#DecodableDerivation`. A case class reads from a mapping, field by field, taking a
      field's default for a key the mapping lacks and raising `Yaml.Error(Absent)` for one
      with no default; a sum type reads its variant from the discriminator field named by
      the `Discriminable in Yaml` in scope (`discriminables.yamlByTypeDiscriminable`). The
      `@name[Yaml](t"full_name")` and bare `@name` annotations rename a field or variant on
      the wire.
  entry #encodable   any type's `Encodable in Yaml`, from its text encoder or by derivation
      Inline and lowest in priority: a type with an `Encodable in Text` encodes as a string
      scalar, and a type with a `Reflection` is derived by `#EncodableDerivation` — a case
      class to a mapping in field order, omitting a field whose encoding is absent (an
      `Unset` or `None`), and a sum type to its variant's mapping with the discriminator
      field added by the `Discriminable in Yaml` in scope. `@name` renames apply on the way
      out as on the way in.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.blockYamlFormatting
      import discriminables.yamlByTypeDiscriminable

      enum Shape:
        case Circle(radius: Double)
        case Square(side: Double)

      (Shape.Circle(2.0): Shape).in[Yaml].show   // radius: 2.0\ntype: Circle\n
      ```
  entry #optional   `Optional[T] is Decodable in Yaml`: as `.option`, yielding `Unset`
      The same three policies, for a field typed `Optional[Int]` (or any `value >: Unset.type`
      which is `Mandatable to inner`): absent and `null` read as `Unset` by default, a
      wrong-typed value is a fault unless `lenientYamlFaults` is imported.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Member(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

      t"name: Eve\nage: null".read[Yaml].as[Member]   // Member(t"Eve", Unset)

      locally:
        import optionalityOptions.strictYamlNulls
        capture[Yaml.Error](t"name: Eve\nage: null".read[Yaml].as[Member]).reason
        // Yaml.Error.Reason.NotType(Yaml.Primitive.Null, Yaml.Primitive.Integer)
      ```
  entry #optionalEncodable   `Optional[T] is Encodable in Yaml`: `Unset` encodes as absent
      An `Unset` field encodes as the absent node, which a derived mapping omits altogether;
      a present value encodes through the inner `Encodable in Yaml`.
  entry #encodableOver   `person.in[Text over Yaml]`: encode to the tree and render, in one step
      The write-side mirror of `read[Person in Yaml]`: the value's `Encodable in Yaml` builds
      the tree and the `Yaml.Formatting` in scope renders it, giving exactly the text `show`
      renders. A type with a `Yaml.Printable` takes `.encodableOverPrinted` instead.
  entry #aggregableIn   `text.read[Person in Yaml]`: parse and decode in one step, through the tree
      The `(value in Yaml) is Aggregable by Text` for any type with a `Decodable in Yaml`:
      the text (or stream) is parsed to a `Yaml` and decoded with `as`, so it needs a
      `Tactic[Parse.Error]` and a `Tactic[Yaml.Error]`. A type with a `Yaml.Parsable` takes
      `.aggregableParsed` instead, which outranks this.
  entry #DecodableDerivation   the wisteria derivation of `Decodable in Yaml` for products and sums
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: `Yaml.decodable` reaches it for any type with a `Reflection`.
        `conjunction` derives a product's decoder — each field read at its own focus, a
        missing field taking its default or registering `Absent`, and construction gated so
        the constructor never runs on sentinel values — and `disjunction` a sum's, through
        the `Discriminable in Yaml` in scope, with `Variant.Error` for an unknown or missing
        discriminator folded into one `Yaml.Error` at the sum's focus.
    entry .conjunction   a product's decoder, from a mapping, field by field
    entry .disjunction   a sum's decoder, by the discriminator field
  entry #EncodableDerivation   the wisteria derivation of `Encodable in Yaml` for products and sums
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: `Yaml.encodable` reaches it for any type with a `Reflection`.
        `conjunction` writes a product as a mapping in field order, omitting absent
        encodings; `disjunction` writes a sum's variant and adds the discriminator field.
    entry .conjunction   a product's encoder, to a mapping
    entry .disjunction   a sum's encoder, with the discriminator added

entry .YamlPath   the address of a node in a document, written `#/a/0/b` with JSON Pointer escaping
  detail
      A `YamlPath` names a node by the keys and indices from the root: `YamlPath()` is the
      whole document (`#`), and `YamlPath()(t"servers")(Prim)(t"port")` is the `port` of the
      first server (`#/servers/0/port`). It is a serpentine path on its own plane, so it is
      built with `/` or the `apply` methods, written with the `yp"…"` interpolator (checked
      as the code compiles), decoded from text with `as[YamlPath]`, and encoded with `encode`.
      Segments use RFC 6901's escaping — `~0` for `~` and `~1` for `/` — so a path is the
      JSON Pointer of the same node when the document is treated as JSON. Only string
      mapping keys are addressable.

      A path is what a `Yaml.Focus` carries, so an accrued decoding error says `#/age`; and
      it is what `locate` takes to find a node's source position in a tracked document.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A document, parsed with positions recorded
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import parsing.trackPositions

            val doc = t"servers:\n  - host: a.example\n    port: 8080\n".read[Yaml]
      frame
        label A path to the first server's port
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import parsing.trackPositions

            val doc = t"servers:\n  - host: a.example\n    port: 8080\n".read[Yaml]
            val path: YamlPath = yp"#/servers/0/port"
            path.encode                                   // t"#/servers/0/port"
      frame
        label Where that node sits in the source
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import parsing.trackPositions

            val doc = t"servers:\n  - host: a.example\n    port: 8080\n".read[Yaml]
            val path: YamlPath = yp"#/servers/0/port"
            doc.locate(path).let(_.describe)              // t"line 3, column 11"
      frame
        label The node itself, navigated and decoded
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import parsing.trackPositions

            val doc = t"servers:\n  - host: a.example\n    port: 8080\n".read[Yaml]
            val path: YamlPath = yp"#/servers/0/port"
            doc.locate(path).let(_.describe)              // t"line 3, column 11"
            doc(t"servers")(0)(t"port").as[Int]           // 8080
      ##
      ```

      Decoding text raises `YamlPath.Error` when it does not begin with `#`, when the
      fragment does not begin with `/`, or when a `~` is not followed by `0` or `1`.
  refer ypsiloid.yp
  refer ypsiloid.Yaml.Focus
  refer ypsiloid.Yaml.positionable
  entry .Plane   the plane of YAML paths: `YamlPath` itself
  entry .navigable   an `Ordinal` as a path segment: its zero-based index as text
      `YamlPath()(Prim)` encodes as `#/0`, so an index is written the way a JSON Pointer
      writes one.
  entry .admissible   any `Ordinal` is an acceptable segment
  entry .admissible2   any `Text` is an acceptable segment; escaping happens on encoding
  entry .filesystem   the path grammar: `/`-separated, `#` as self, `..` as parent, RFC 6901 escaping
      Named `YAML`. `escape` turns `~` into `~0` and `/` into `~1`, and `unescape` reverses
      it, so a key containing either character is addressable.
  entry .interpolable   the given behind `yp"…"`; see `ypsiloid.yp`
  entry .decodable   reads a path from text: `#`, `#/`, or `#/a/b`
      `t"#/a/b".as[YamlPath]`, raising `YamlPath.Error` with the character offset of the
      fault: `ExpectedHash` at 0 for text not beginning with `#`, `ExpectedSlash` at 1 for a
      fragment not beginning with `/`, and `BadEscape` at the `~` for a malformed escape.
      Segments are unescaped as they are read, and empty segments are dropped. The path also
      has an anonymous `Encodable in Text` given in the companion, so `path.encode` renders
      `#/a/b` (and `#` for the root) with the segments escaped.
  entry .unsafeParse   decodes a path already proved valid, with no tactic
      For the `yp"…"` interpolator, which has parsed the literal while compiling: the
      `Unsafe` token asserts that the runtime re-parse cannot fail.
  entry .divisible   `path / t"key"`: a path extended by a text segment
  entry .divisible2   `path / ordinal`: a path extended by an index segment
  entry .Error   raised when text is not a valid YAML path
    detail
        Error 546: _the YAML path was not valid because `reason`_. Raised by `.decodable`
        and reported, at the exact character, by the `yp"…"` interpolator as the code
        compiles. `offset` is the index within the text where the fault was found.
    entry #reason   `ExpectedHash`, `ExpectedSlash` or `BadEscape`
    entry #offset   the character index of the fault within the text
    entry .Reason   the three ways a path can be malformed
      detail
          `ExpectedHash` (number 2): _a YAML path must begin with '#'_. `ExpectedSlash`
          (number 3): _a YAML path fragment must begin with '/'_. `BadEscape` (number 4): _a
          '~' in a YAML path must be followed by '0' or '1'_.
      entry .communicable   renders a reason as its message
  entry #path   the underlying serpentine `Path on YamlPath`
  entry #apply   `path(t"key")` or `path(ordinal)`: the path extended by one segment
      The same as `/`, as a method: `YamlPath()(t"servers")(Prim)(t"port")`.

entry .y   the `y"…"` interpolator: a YAML literal, checked as the code compiles, with `$`-holes; also a pattern
    `y"name: $name\nage: $age"` is a `Yaml` document whose text is parsed while the code
    compiles — a malformed literal is a compile error whose highlight is the exact bad span
    of the template — and whose `$`-holes are filled at runtime. A hole in value position
    takes any `Yaml` (or a value with an `Encodable in Yaml`, encoded through it); a hole
    inside a plain scalar (`greeting: hello $name`) splices the value's text into the
    string; and `$xs*`, as the last element of a sequence, spreads an `Iterable`'s items
    into it (each encoded through its `Encodable in Yaml`). A value with no encoder, or a
    spread which is not last or not an `Iterable`, is a compile error highlighted at the
    hole. The result carries no positions.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    val name = t"Alice"
    val age = 30

    y"""
      name: $name
      age: $age
    """.as[Person]                        // Person(t"Alice", 30)

    val more: List[Int] = List(2, 3)
    y"[$age, $more*]".as[List[Int]]       // List(30, 2, 3)
    ```

    In a pattern, `case y"name: $n" =>` matches a document whose structure is the
    literal's and binds `n` to the node at the hole, as a `Yaml`; a literal with no holes
    matches by structural equality.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    t"name: Alice".read[Yaml] match
      case y"name: $n" => n.as[Text]   // t"Alice"
      case _           => t""
    ```

entry .yp   the `yp"…"` interpolator: a `YamlPath` literal, checked as the code compiles
    `yp"#/servers/0/port"` is the `YamlPath` of that text, decoded while the code compiles
    through `YamlPath`'s own decoder, so a malformed path is a compile error pointing at the
    offending character: `yp"/foo"` is rejected at the `/` (_a YAML path must begin with
    '#'_), and `yp"#/a~2b"` at the `~`. The interpolator takes no `$`-substitutions.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    yp"#/servers/0/port".encode   // t"#/servers/0/port"
    yp"#".encode                  // t"#"
    ```

entry .formatting   how a document is rendered: one given, imported by name
  detail
      `show`, `in[Text over Yaml]`, `Yaml.emit` and the HTTP givens need a `Yaml.Formatting`
      in scope, and `import soundness.*` brings none in, so the choice is always explicit:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.blockYamlFormatting

      t"{a: [1, 2], b: {}}".read[Yaml].show   // a:\n  - 1\n  - 2\nb: {}\n
      ```

      Block style is the only formatting at present; the package exists so that a flow-style
      or option-carrying formatting can be added beside it without changing any call site.
      In the `soundness` umbrella the package merges with the other formats' `formatting`
      packages (`indentedJsonFormatting`, …).
  entry .blockYamlFormatting   two-space-indented block style, with `{}` and `[]` for empty collections
      The default and only `Yaml.Formatting`. Its output is chosen to re-parse to the same
      tree: scalars are written so the parser resolves them back to the same kind, and a
      string is plain only when it cannot be mistaken for anything else.

entry .optionalityOptions   how an `Optional` or `Option` field reads a missing key, a `null`, or a bad value
  detail
      Three independent policies, each a pair of givens of which one is imported by name;
      the defaults (from distillate) are lenient absence, lenient nulls and strict faults.
      They govern `Yaml.optional`, `Yaml.option` and the direct `Yaml.Field.optional` and
      `Yaml.Field.option` alike. In the umbrella the package merges with the other formats'
      `optionalityOptions`, so the names carry `Yaml` to stay distinct.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The defaults: a null reads as Unset, a wrong-typed value is an error
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Member(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

            t"name: Eve\nage: null".read[Yaml].as[Member]        // Member(t"Eve", Unset)
            t"name: Eve".read[Yaml].as[Member]                   // Member(t"Eve", Unset)
            capture[Yaml.Error](t"name: Eve\nage: abc".read[Yaml].as[Member]).reason
            // Yaml.Error.Reason.NotType(Yaml.Primitive.Str, Yaml.Primitive.Integer)
      frame
        label Strict nulls: a null is handed to the inner decoder, which rejects it
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.strictYamlNulls

            case class Member(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

            capture[Yaml.Error](t"name: Eve\nage: null".read[Yaml].as[Member]).reason
            // Yaml.Error.Reason.NotType(Yaml.Primitive.Null, Yaml.Primitive.Integer)
      frame
        label Strict absence: the key must be present, even if null
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.strictYamlAbsence

            case class Member(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

            capture[Yaml.Error](t"name: Eve".read[Yaml].as[Member]).reason
            // Yaml.Error.Reason.Absent
      frame
        label Lenient faults: a value the inner decoder rejects reads as Unset
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.lenientYamlFaults

            case class Member(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

            t"name: Eve\nage: abc".read[Yaml].as[Member]         // Member(t"Eve", Unset)
      ##
      ```
  entry .strictYamlAbsence   a missing key is `Yaml.Error(Absent)`, even for an `Optional` field
  entry .lenientYamlAbsence   a missing key reads as `Unset` or `None`: the default
  entry .strictYamlNulls   a `null` is handed to the inner decoder, which fails for anything but `Unit`
  entry .lenientYamlNulls   a `null` reads as `Unset` or `None`: the default
  entry .strictYamlFaults   a value the inner decoder rejects is an error: the default
  entry .lenientYamlFaults   a value the inner decoder rejects reads as `Unset` or `None`
      The inner decode runs under `tactic.tolerate`, so under an accruing strategy the fault
      is not registered either.

entry .discriminables   how a sum type's variant is named on the wire: by a `type` or a `kind` field
  detail
      Encoding or decoding an enumeration or sealed hierarchy needs a `Discriminable in
      Yaml` in scope, which `import soundness.*` does not bring; import one of these by
      name, or build one for another field with `Yaml.discriminatedUnion`. The variant's
      name (or its `@name`) is written as a string scalar under the field, alongside the
      variant's own fields; on decoding the field is read and then stripped before the
      variant's decoder runs. The umbrella merges this package with the other formats'.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import discriminables.yamlByTypeDiscriminable

      enum Shape:
        case Circle(radius: Double)
        case Square(side: Double)

      t"type: Square\nside: 7.0".read[Yaml].as[Shape]   // Shape.Square(7.0)
      ```
  entry .yamlByTypeDiscriminable   the variant's name under a `type` field
  entry .yamlByKindDiscriminable   the variant's name under a `kind` field

entry .dynamicAccess   the given which enables `doc.field` reads and `doc.field = value` updates
  detail
      `Yaml#selectDynamic`, `#applyDynamic`, `#update`, `#updateDynamic` and the `Yaml.lens`
      given are all gated on a `Yaml is Dynamical`, so that a misspelt member name is a
      compile error rather than a silent field lookup. Import `dynamicYaml` to grant it to a
      scope, or wrap a block in `dynamically[Yaml]:` (or `dynamically:` for every format)
      to grant it there alone.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicYaml

      val doc = t"outer: {inner: {n: 7}}".read[Yaml]
      doc.outer.inner.n.as[Int]   // 7
      ```
  entry .dynamicYaml   `Yaml is Dynamical`: dynamic member access on documents, for the scope it is imported into
      An erased given: it costs nothing at runtime and exists only to be in scope.

entry .conversions   a conversion letting any encodable value be assigned through a lens
  detail
      `doc.lens(_.field = value)` wants a `Yaml` on the right; with `encodableToYaml`
      imported, any value with an `Encodable in Yaml` is converted on the spot, so
      `doc.lens(_.age = 31)` reads as it should instead of `doc.lens(_.age = 31.in[Yaml])`.
      The conversion is scoped to the import.
  entry .encodableToYaml   `Conversion[T, Yaml]` for any `T` with an `Encodable in Yaml`

entry .postables   a document as an HTTP request body, for the `ypsiloid.http` source set
  detail
      Imported by name, as every telekinesis `Postable` choice is:
      `import postables.yamlPostable` lets a `Yaml` be the body of a `post` or `put`.
  entry .yamlPostable   `Yaml is Postable` with the `application/yaml; charset=UTF-8` media type
      The body is the rendered text (`show`) in the `Codepage` in scope, so a `Codepage` and
      a `Yaml.Formatting` are both needed.

entry .servables   a document as an HTTP response body, for the `ypsiloid.http` source set
  detail
      `import servables.yamlServable` lets a handler respond with a `Yaml` value; the
      response is the rendered text as a fixed-length body with the `application/yaml;
      charset=UTF-8` media type.
  entry .yamlServable   `Yaml is Servable` with the `application/yaml; charset=UTF-8` media type
      Needs a `Codepage` and a `Yaml.Formatting` in scope.

entry .construables   reading an `application/yaml` body as a `Yaml`, for the `ypsiloid.http` source set
  detail
      `import construables.yamlConstruable` tells gesticulate's content negotiation that a
      body of media type `application/yaml` is constructed as a `Yaml`, so a request or
      response body of that type parses on arrival. Not available on Scala.js.
  entry .yamlConstruable   `"application/yaml" is Construable to Yaml`

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `y"…"` and `yp"…"`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home. `y"…"`
      joins the literal's parts with a sentinel marker, parses the result with the real
      parser, and recognises the sentinel in the tree as a hole; a parse error's offset is
      mapped back through the parts to the source span of the template.
  entry .interpolator   the macro behind `y"…"` as an expression
  entry .extractor   the macro behind `y"…"` as a pattern
  entry .yamlPath   the macro behind `yp"…"`: decodes the literal while compiling and reports the fault's character
